{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>It is not because of your multitude among all the peoples that YHWH desired you and chose you.</b> This expression needs interpretation, for the difficulty is: whence could one even say 'because of your multitude YHWH desired you,' when in truth they are the fewest? And further, what reason did he give by saying ״כִּי מֵאַהֲבַת ה׳ אֶתְכֶם״ — he gave the reason for the love by means of love itself? And there is yet another difficulty: ״חָשַׁק בָּכֶם וַיִּבְחַר בָּכֶם״ — why do I need two instances of 'in you'? It would have sufficed to say ״חָשַׁק וַיִּבְחַר ה׳ בָּכֶם״. And what is the difference between חָשַׁק (desire) and בָּחַר (chose)? And there is yet a subtle point: in the word ״מֵרֻבְּכֶם״ he included the people and the number in a single word, whereas in saying ״כִּי אַתֶּם הַמְעַט״ he separated between them." "And the interpretation of all this is: YHWH saw Israel that in truth they are not perfect from every side, for they are a stiff-necked people — only that they are better than the other nations, for they have a greater abundance of merits than the peoples, and fewer sins than all the peoples. Now you might suppose that, on the one hand, because they have a certain abundance of merits, YHWH desired them — for 'desire' is something one says regarding a beloved thing; and on the other hand, because they have a paucity of sins, He chose them, like one choosing the lesser of evils in its smallness. But lest one err and say that on this account their deeds are acceptable to HaShem, blessed be He, it teaches us: ״כִּי מֵאַהֲבַת ה׳ אֶתְכֶם״ — you yourselves are acceptable, but your deeds are not acceptable, and neither desire nor choice was caused by them. And this is what it says: ״לֹא מֵרֻבְּכֶם מִכָּל הָעַמִּים״ — since He is speaking of the abundance of merits that are attached to them, He included the people and the number in a single word, to their honor. And He said that YHWH did not desire you on account of this slight abundance of merits that you have over all the peoples. ״וַיִּבְחַר בָּכֶם״ also refers back to the word ״לֹא״, as if He said that He also did not choose you because you are the fewest — because you have fewer sins than all the peoples, like one choosing the lesser of evils in its smallness. And to their honor He separated the number from the people, so as not to attach the sins to them in total binding but rather separated them from them. It was not on account of these two factors that YHWH desired and chose you, for so small a thing does not tip the scale, given that in truth your deeds are not acceptable. ״כִּי מֵאַהֲבַת ה׳ אֶתְכֶם״ — for you yourselves are acceptable on account of your stock, ״וּמִשָּׁמְרוֹ אֶת הַשְּׁבוּעָה״ etc. — meaning, the merit of the fathers is what stood for you. And this is alluded to in the word ״אֶתְכֶם״, to say: you alone are acceptable, but your deeds are not acceptable, as it is written (Deuteronomy 9:5) ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ״ etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:39.943234633Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}